CORE
main.cpp
--cpp23 --show-goto-functions
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^.*std::optional<signed_int>::transform.*$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^PARSING ERROR$
--
C++23 std::optional monadic operation `transform`
(`o.transform([](int x){ return x * 2; })`).

This verifies the frontend instantiates and invokes
`std::optional<int>::transform` with the lambda.

Verified at the goto-program level rather than via full BMC: a
correctly-elaborated `std::optional<int>` plus its monadic
`transform` pulls in the entire `_Optional_base`/`_Optional_payload`
instantiation graph, which exceeds symbolic-execution memory limits
(this is why cpp17_optional_basic / cpp20_optional_basic are also
checked at the goto level).  The goto-level check confirms
`transform` is actually instantiated and called.
